  "executive_summary": "This study addresses the critical gap in understanding the Solid-Electrolyte Interphase (SEI) in lithium-ion batteries, a complex passivating layer that remains poorly characterized due to the limitations of existing simulation methods. Traditional Density Functional Theory (DFT) methods are too slow for the necessary length and time scales, while classical force fields lack the accuracy for complex redox reactions. The authors propose a novel approach using a multitask machine learning force field (MLFF) based on the QRNN architecture to simulate SEI formation and rank candidate structures by predicted energy.\n\nThe methodology involves a combination of classical sampling and active learning. Initial SEI candidate structures were generated using the Schrödinger SEI Simulator with OPLS4 molecular dynamics, starting with 1 M LiPF6 in ethylene carbonate (EC). These candidates were then annealed using a reactive QRNN model under extreme conditions (400 K, 1000 atm) to ensure stability and reaction completion. The model was trained on a diverse dataset including periodic and non-periodic systems, labeled with PBE-D3 and wB97X-D3BJ/def2-TZVPD levels of theory, as well as a supplemental SPICE dataset for general organic chemistry to shape the latent space.\n\nKey results demonstrate that the MLFF can stably simulate complex redox reactions, producing the first set of SEI candidate structures ranked by energy. Statistical analysis of the bonding in these structures reveals that P-F and Li-O bonds are associated with more stable configurations, while C-O and Li-F bonds correlate with less stability. The study validates the model against surface reaction energy barriers, showing reasonable accuracy. The authors conclude that this approach provides a quantitative foundation for understanding SEI chemistry and suggests future work on comparing multiple electrolyte formulations and applying other MLFF architectures like Nequip and MACE. This work represents a significant step toward using machine learning to model complex battery interphases, offering a pathway to optimize SEI composition for next-generation batteries.",
